'use strict';
const C=require('../index'),questions=require('./rest-questions');
const SID='rest-api-design',lessons=[];
const section=(heading,a,b,extra={})=>({heading,paragraphs:[a,b],...extra});
const json=value=>({snippet:{lang:'json',code:JSON.stringify(value,null,2)}});
function add(id,comp,title,introduction,sections,code,expect,problem,steps,explanation,mistakes,takeaways,references){const o=C.competency(SID+'.'+comp);lessons.push({id,module:o.module,version:1,title,minutes:45,objectives:[{id:SID+'.'+comp,text:o.competency.text}],prerequisites:lessons.length?[lessons.at(-1).id]:[],prerequisite_note:lessons.length?'Use the preceding lessons’ resource, representation and contract vocabulary. Earlier lessons remain accessible for review.':'Begin after HTTP, Networking and Web Architecture. That prerequisite explains methods, statuses, headers, cookies and transport boundaries; this course applies them to business API design. Familiar Python notation is used only to verify offline examples; all assessed work is MCQ-based.',introduction,sections,worked:{lang:'python',code,expect,problem,steps,explanation},mistakes,takeaways,references,reuse:[],quiz:questions[id].slice(0,3).map(q=>q.id)});}
add('rest-resources','m1.c1','Turn a business workflow into resource contracts',
 'A shop needs customers to place orders, inspect them and request cancellation without deleting their history. Before choosing a framework, define what an order represents, who can act on it and which state transitions the business permits. A well-designed API exposes that contract rather than a collection of database operations.',[
 section('Resource identity is separate from a representation','A resource is something the API identifies, such as an order, enrollment or cancellation request. A representation is a transferable description of its current state, often JSON. The same resource can have a compact list representation and a richer detail representation. An identifier belongs to the API contract and need not reveal a database table or internal primary-key strategy.','Resource-oriented URLs commonly use nouns: GET /orders/ord-7 reads an order; POST /orders submits creation to a collection; PATCH /orders/ord-7 applies a documented partial modification. PUT means creation/replacement at the known target, not a universal “update any fields” shortcut. Operations such as cancellation can be modelled as a cancellation resource with POST /orders/ord-7/cancellations, preserving the order and its audit history.'),
 section('REST is more than noun-shaped endpoints','REST describes architectural constraints including client-server separation, stateless interactions, cacheability, a uniform interface, layered systems and optional code on demand. Statelessness means each request carries the information needed for its processing without relying on one server instance’s conversational history. It does not mean the service cannot store orders or use shared authentication state.','A uniform interface includes resource identification, manipulation through representations, self-descriptive messages and hypermedia-driven available actions. A JSON API with noun URLs but no such controls is often better described as a resource-oriented HTTP API than proof of full REST conformance. Returning an allowed cancellation link can reduce clients hard-coding state-machine rules, but the server still enforces the transition and authorization when invoked.'),
 section('Design responses around client work and permissions','For a completed creation, a typical contract returns 201, a Location identifying the resource and a useful representation. A queued long-running operation can return 202 with a status resource; it must not imply completion. A compact order list should not embed every customer secret and shipment event. Separate bounded summaries from detail or related-resource requests.','Authentication establishes the actor; object-level authorization decides which order that actor may read or cancel. A path under /customers/7 does not establish ownership. Cancellation may conflict with a shipped state even for the owner. Document a consistent conflict response and enforce the rule atomically in the service; the offline model below only demonstrates the resulting contract.',json({id:'ord-7',status:'placed',links:{self:'/orders/ord-7',cancel:'/orders/ord-7/cancellations'}}))],
 'import json\norder = {"id": "ord-7", "owner_id": 3, "status": "placed"}\nactor = 3\nif actor != order["owner_id"]:\n    status, body = 403, {"code": "forbidden"}\nelif order["status"] != "placed":\n    status, body = 409, {"code": "cancellation_conflict"}\nelse:\n    status, body = 201, {"id": "cancel-1", "order_id": order["id"], "status": "requested"}\nprint(status)\nprint(json.dumps(body, sort_keys=True))\nprint(order["status"])','201\n{"id": "cancel-1", "order_id": "ord-7", "status": "requested"}\nplaced',
 'Design a cancellation-request response while retaining the original order record.',['Check the synthetic actor before applying a business transition rule.','Create a cancellation-request representation when the order is eligible.','Verify that request acceptance has not silently deleted or marked the order cancelled.'],'The new request is created, while the order remains placed until the documented workflow completes. This model is not an implementation of transactional cancellation or payment reversal.',
 ['A noun URL alone does not prove full REST conformance.','Deleting an order to mean cancellation can destroy required business history.','A returned action link is guidance, not permission to skip server checks.'],['Identify resources, representations and state transitions separately.','Choose methods and statuses from the operation contract.','Expose only authorized, appropriately sized representations.'],['http-semantics']);
add('rest-pagination','m1.c2','Design bounded search and stable pagination',
 'An inventory client wants products in a category, sorted by price, without downloading a million rows. Filtering, ordering, limits and continuation need one coherent contract. Correct pagination is as much about stable meaning as it is about response size.',[
 section('Make query behavior explicit and bounded','GET /products?category=books&sort=price&limit=20 describes selection and presentation without changing products. Define allowed filters, sort fields/directions, default limit and maximum limit. Bind filter values through the database driver, and allowlist sort identifiers rather than concatenating arbitrary query text into ORDER BY. An API filter does not replace tenant or object authorization.','Path parameters identify a resource, query parameters configure retrieval, and bodies carry submitted representations where the method contract supports them. Reject or document repeated and unknown query parameters rather than letting different layers interpret them differently. Large unbounded result sets can exhaust both server and mobile-client resources even if the query is otherwise valid.'),
 section('Choose offset or cursor from the access pattern','Offset pagination is simple for bounded stable datasets and numbered navigation, but inserts/deletes before an offset can shift later pages. Keyset pagination resumes after a complete ordered key, such as (price,id), using a unique tie-breaker. It avoids ordinary offset-shift duplication for that traversal; concurrent changes to sort values can still affect results unless snapshot semantics are defined.','A cursor is a continuation token whose internal layout clients should not depend on. Base64 encoding makes a value convenient to transport; it does not encrypt, authenticate or authorize it. Validate cursor structure, bind it to the applicable filters/tenant/order and protect it against tampering where the contract needs that. Always enforce authorization independently of a decoded token.'),
 section('Expose continuation without forcing expensive totals','A list envelope can contain items and next_cursor or a next link. Define the end-of-list value explicitly, such as null. An exact total on every page can be expensive at large scale; offer an optional exact count, clearly labelled estimate or a separate count operation when requirements permit. Do not label an approximate count as billing truth.','Index choices and query plans support common filter/order combinations but belong to deeper database study. At the design level, bound work and document which combinations are supported. The fixture sorts a tiny list and resumes after one complete key; it does not prove production performance or provide a secure cursor codec.',json({items:[{id:11,price_cents:500},{id:12,price_cents:500}],next_cursor:'opaque-continuation-token'}))],
 'rows = [{"id": 11, "price": 500}, {"id": 12, "price": 500}, {"id": 13, "price": 700}]\nordered = sorted(rows, key=lambda r: (r["price"], r["id"]))\nafter = (500, 11)\npage = [r for r in ordered if (r["price"], r["id"]) > after][:2]\nprint([r["id"] for r in page])\nprint(len(page))','[12, 13]\n2',
 'Resume an ascending product traversal after a price/id pair without skipping another product at the same price.',['Sort by both price and unique id.','Compare the complete continuation pair, not price alone.','Apply a bounded result size after selecting later records.'],'Product 12 remains because its id breaks the equal-price tie. The tuple is explanatory state, not a public secure cursor or a guarantee against concurrent price edits.',
 ['Sorting only by a nonunique price leaves ambiguous boundaries.','Base64 is not cursor encryption or access control.','Exact totals and unbounded limits can dominate latency.'],['Document filter, sort and limit rules together.','Use a complete stable traversal key.','Distinguish continuation, authorization, snapshot consistency and performance.'],['http-semantics']);
add('rest-errors','m1.c3','Make failures consistent, useful and private',
 'A checkout client must distinguish a malformed request, a denied action and a temporary dependency problem. If every failure returns 200 with a different string, clients and support tools have to guess. A stable error contract lets them choose a useful next step without exposing internal data.',[
 section('Combine status with stable machine-readable details','Use meaningful HTTP status semantics and a stable application code. A malformed request can be 400; absent/invalid authentication normally uses 401 with an applicable challenge; refusal can use 403; a state conflict can use 409. A syntactically valid but invalid instruction can use 422 under the documented validation policy. Different teams should not invent incompatible envelopes for the same public API.','Human-readable messages can evolve or be translated, so clients should not branch on exact English prose. A problem-details-style envelope can include type, title, status, detail and an occurrence reference, with documented application extensions such as code or field errors. Define the fields and media type accurately; an example shape is not a claim that every error is a standards-certified implementation.'),
 section('Give actionable validation feedback without leaking existence','Field-level errors should identify public input paths and constraints, such as quantity must be positive. Multiple independent safe errors can be returned together to avoid a submit/fix cycle. Do not echo passwords, tokens, unrestricted request bodies or private database values. Keep internal stack traces behind controlled diagnostics.','A consistent 404 policy for nonexistent and unauthorized private objects can reduce enumeration when the product’s disclosure rules require it. Apply the policy consistently across error body, timing and related endpoints as feasible. It does not replace permission checks; it only governs what an unauthorized caller learns about a failed check.'),
 section('Represent uncertainty instead of asserting a rollback','A gateway timeout can leave a payment’s final outcome uncertain. Return a safe operation reference/status path when available, with documented follow-up and retry rules. Do not return “definitely failed” merely because one intermediary stopped waiting. A 202 job resource is appropriate only when the system actually accepted responsibility for tracked work.','Test errors as contracts: status, public code, field paths, content type and prohibited sensitive fields. A nonsecret request id helps staff correlate the incident without becoming an access credential. The fixture constructs one response and checks a forbidden field is absent; it does not exercise a real gateway.',json({type:'about:blank',title:'Conflict',status:409,code:'out_of_stock',detail:'Requested quantity is unavailable.',request_id:'req-demo-4'}))],
 'import json\nerror = {"type": "about:blank", "title": "Conflict", "status": 409, "code": "out_of_stock", "request_id": "req-demo-4"}\nassert error["status"] == 409\nassert "stack" not in error and "token" not in error\nprint(json.dumps(error, sort_keys=True))','{"code": "out_of_stock", "request_id": "req-demo-4", "status": 409, "title": "Conflict", "type": "about:blank"}',
 'Build a stable public conflict envelope without exposing internal diagnostics.',['Choose status from the state conflict rather than returning successful 200.','Include a machine-readable code and nonsecret occurrence reference.','Assert that sensitive diagnostic fields are absent from this representation.'],'The shape supports client branching and support correlation. The model verifies only its declared fields, not a complete problem-details or production error-handler implementation.',
 ['A 200 failure envelope can mislead clients and monitoring.','English text is a fragile machine-readable error discriminator.','A timeout alone cannot establish that a write rolled back.'],['Define a consistent status and error schema.','Give safe field feedback and useful correlation.','Treat disclosure and uncertain outcomes as explicit design decisions.'],['http-semantics']);
add('rest-validation','m2.c1','Validate syntax, shape, meaning and write permissions',
 'A university enrollment form checks its fields in the browser, but a caller can submit requests without that form. The server must enforce the data contract at its own boundary. Correct validation separates parseability from meaningful and authorized instructions.',[
 section('Validation has several layers','First bound request size and parse the advertised representation. Valid JSON only establishes syntax. Next validate shape: required fields, types, lengths, formats and unknown-field policy. Then check semantic rules such as a positive quantity or end_date not preceding start_date. Finally apply actor/object permissions and state constraints at the operation boundary.','A number, string, Boolean and null are distinct JSON types. Coercing every value silently can turn an accidental input into a different instruction. In Python, bool is a subclass of int, so a toy integer check must account for that if JSON true should not be accepted as quantity one. Real production schemas and parsers need a specified, tested policy rather than accidental language coercion.'),
 section('Prevent overposting and resource exhaustion','Allowlist client-writable properties. A request containing is_admin_approved must not overwrite a server-managed approval field merely because it is well-formed JSON. A field may be valid for an administrator but forbidden for a learner; a schema alone is not the full permission model. Keep output projections separate from writeable input schemas.','Bound array size, nesting, text length, total bytes and operational work. An import endpoint should reject or route oversized batches through a designed asynchronous flow before retaining millions of records in memory. Browser validation is useful feedback but cannot enforce these service-side boundaries.'),
 section('Specify how clients repair invalid input','Use the documented 400/422 policy consistently for shape and semantic failures. Return public field errors where safe, including multiple independent issues. Do not echo a secret value merely to explain that its format was invalid. For a changing business state, a valid request can still produce a conflict after validation passes.','The example intentionally checks a tiny subset of an order input contract using ordinary code. It is not a general JSON Schema engine, authorization service or concurrency-safe stock allocation. Its purpose is to make the distinctions and expected outcomes explicit.',json({product_id:'prod-7',quantity:2}))],
 'def validate(payload):\n    errors = []\n    if set(payload) - {"product_id", "quantity"}:\n        errors.append("unknown_field")\n    if not isinstance(payload.get("product_id"), str) or not payload["product_id"]:\n        errors.append("product_id")\n    q = payload.get("quantity")\n    if type(q) is not int or not 1 <= q <= 20:\n        errors.append("quantity")\n    return errors\nprint(validate({"product_id": "prod-7", "quantity": 2}))\nprint(validate({"product_id": "prod-7", "quantity": True, "is_admin_approved": True}))', '[]\n[\'unknown_field\', \'quantity\']',
 'Distinguish an allowed order input from Boolean quantity and server-field overposting.',['Check the public property allowlist.','Require a nonempty product identifier and a bounded exact integer quantity.','Compare valid and intentionally invalid synthetic requests.'],'The Boolean is rejected instead of being treated as integer one, and the managed approval field is not accepted. A real service still authorizes the product/order and handles inventory state atomically.',
 ['Valid JSON does not prove valid business meaning.','Copying arbitrary fields into a model permits overposting.','Client validation and permissive coercion cannot replace server enforcement.'],['Validate size, syntax, shape, semantics and permissions deliberately.','Separate writable inputs from internal/output models.','Test invalid cases as well as successful ones.'],['openapi','http-semantics']);
add('rest-data-contracts','m2.c1','Define money, dates and external-input boundaries',
 'A payment amount that looks harmless in JSON can become an accounting defect if currency, scale or rounding is unspecified. A callback URL can become a network access instruction. A professional contract defines the meaning and operational limits of its data, not only its field names.',[
 section('Use unambiguous representations for important values','A contract can represent money as integer minor units plus currency, or an exact decimal string with a declared scale and rounding policy. A bare binary floating-point number without those rules is a poor financial promise. Different currencies can have different minor-unit rules, and a format change from a string to a number can break clients.','Dates and timestamps also need distinct meaning. A local date such as an appointment day is not an instant; an instant needs a defined timezone/offset representation and parsing policy. Validate relationships between fields, including date range order. A syntactically valid timestamp is not proof that an appointment is within the actor’s allowed schedule.'),
 section('Treat a supplied URL as an operational capability','A server that fetches callback_url can be induced to contact internal or special network endpoints. Validate the allowed scheme, destination policy, resolved addresses, redirects and connection behavior at the actual fetch boundary. A string beginning with https is not sufficient protection; DNS and redirects can change where a request goes.','Where possible, use a constrained registered destination rather than arbitrary caller-controlled URLs, with reviewed egress controls and time/size limits. The course does not probe any internal service or teach live exploitation. The validation lesson focuses on defensive boundaries; complete network security implementation belongs to the API Security subtrack.'),
 section('Publish missing-value and compatibility rules','Define required versus optional, absent versus null, default values and unknown-property handling. Explicit JSON null is not the same as an omitted field in a patch contract. Make enum evolution and range limits visible in documentation, and verify that clients tolerate changes you intend to classify as compatible.','The worked amount parser accepts only a fixed decimal format for this synthetic USD example. It deliberately rejects excess scale rather than silently rounding, and excludes nonfinite or negative inputs through its narrow grammar. This is a teaching decision, not a universal multi-currency payment validator.',json({amount:'12.30',currency:'USD',appointment_at:'2026-12-01T09:00:00+05:00'}))],
 'import re\nfrom decimal import Decimal\ndef cents(value):\n    if not isinstance(value, str) or not re.fullmatch(r"[0-9]+\\.[0-9]{2}", value):\n        return "invalid_amount"\n    amount = Decimal(value)\n    return int(amount * 100)\nprint(cents("12.30"))\nprint(cents("12.345"))\nprint(cents("NaN"))','1230\ninvalid_amount\ninvalid_amount',
 'Convert a declared two-decimal payment string into exact minor units without an implicit floating-point conversion.',['Check the deliberately narrow decimal-string grammar.','Use decimal arithmetic before converting to integer cents.','Test excess scale and a nonfinite textual value.'],'The valid amount maps exactly to 1230 cents. Rejected values need safe validation feedback; actual payment authorization, maximum amount and currency policy are separate checks.',
 ['A numeric-looking JSON value does not define currency or rounding.','https syntax alone does not make a server fetch destination safe.','Absent and null can have different update meanings.'],['Document representation units, precision and time semantics.','Validate operational capabilities at their use boundary.','Make defaults and missing-value rules part of the contract.'],['openapi','http-semantics']);
add('rest-openapi-versioning','m2.c2','Document and evolve a testable API contract',
 'A mobile app may remain installed for months after the API changes. Documentation and compatibility are therefore operational contracts, not just a page written after code. OpenAPI can describe the public operations so tooling and tests can compare intended behavior with actual behavior.',[
 section('Describe operations and schemas precisely','An OpenAPI document declares its specification version, API information, paths, operations, parameters, request bodies, responses and reusable components. A path placeholder such as {id} must correspond to a required path parameter. Document error responses as well as success, and declare authentication requirements with the appropriate security schemes and operation policies.','OpenAPI 3.1 uses JSON Schema-aligned schema behavior. A required property must appear; permitting null is a separate type/schema rule. A document stored as JSON can describe non-JSON payloads through content media types. Static JSON parsing checks syntax only; it does not establish complete OpenAPI conformance or prove an implementation honors the schema.'),
 section('Classify compatibility from real client behavior','Removing or renaming a response field, adding a required request field, narrowing an accepted range or changing a field type can break existing clients. Adding an optional response field is often compatible with tolerant readers but can break clients that reject unknown properties. New enum values can break exhaustive client branches even when the schema change looks additive.','For a nonbreaking rename, retain the old field during a defined migration and document how new and old fields coexist. For an unavoidable break, expose a versioned contract with a realistic transition period. Avoid endless independently maintained copies: share internal behavior where possible and retire versions using measured client usage and a documented policy.'),
 section('Keep documents, examples and consumer behavior aligned','Validate contract examples and responses in CI, and compare schema changes for compatibility. Consumer contract tests can catch a separately released mobile client’s assumptions earlier than a production incident. A version prefix does not by itself keep behavior compatible or guarantee all actual responses match the file.','Deprecation/Sunset information and migration documentation can help clients plan a change, but a header does not migrate them automatically. The fixture below inspects one minimal OpenAPI structure and a path-parameter rule; it is explicitly not a full standards validator. A real pipeline should run a compatible full validator and implementation tests before release.',json({openapi:'3.1.1',info:{title:'Orders contract',version:'1.0.0'},paths:{'/orders/{id}':{get:{parameters:[{name:'id',in:'path',required:true,schema:{type:'string'}}],responses:{'200':{description:'Order representation'},'404':{description:'Not found or undisclosed'}}}}}}))],
 'spec = {"openapi": "3.1.1", "info": {"title": "Orders", "version": "1.0.0"}, "paths": {"/orders/{id}": {"get": {"parameters": [{"name": "id", "in": "path", "required": True, "schema": {"type": "string"}}], "responses": {"200": {"description": "Order"}, "404": {"description": "Not found"}}}}}}\nop = spec["paths"]["/orders/{id}"]["get"]\nassert any(p["name"] == "id" and p["in"] == "path" and p["required"] for p in op["parameters"])\nprint(spec["openapi"])\nprint(sorted(op["responses"]))', '3.1.1\n[\'200\', \'404\']',
 'Inspect a minimal versioned OpenAPI operation and its required path parameter.',['Locate the GET operation under the templated resource path.','Check that id is declared as a required path parameter.','Read the documented success and not-found response keys.'],'The assertions establish the selected structure only. They do not validate every OpenAPI rule, execute a live route or certify backward compatibility.',
 ['Parsing JSON is not complete OpenAPI validation.','An additive enum value can break an exhaustive client.','A version number cannot repair undocumented incompatible behavior.'],['Describe inputs, outputs, errors and security together.','Assess compatibility from consumer assumptions.','Test contracts and provide a deliberate migration path.'],['openapi']);
add('rest-idempotency','m3.c1','Specify duplicate-request behavior durably',
 'A customer submits a payment and the response is lost. The client needs to recover that one logical operation, not create a new charge. HTTP Foundations introduced method semantics; this lesson designs the operation record, payload checks and race handling that make a business retry contract credible.',[
 section('Bind one operation identity to one intended request','Scope an idempotency key to the authenticated tenant/actor and operation. Store a canonical payload fingerprint with the key and preserve the agreed response or operation status. An identical retry can replay or query that result; the same key with different payment details must produce the documented conflict instead of silently reusing the wrong charge.','A key is not an authorization credential and a new random key on every retry does not deduplicate the original work. Define retention from the retry/reconciliation window and provider guarantees, not an arbitrary ten-minute cache rule. Define in-progress behavior so two concurrent identical requests do not both execute before either records completion.'),
 section('Coordinate local records with remote effects','A local “insert key, call provider, save result” sequence has a crash gap if the provider charges but the local result is not saved. Use the provider’s own stable operation/idempotency reference where available and reconcile ambiguous outcomes. Local bookkeeping alone cannot promise exactly-once execution in an independent remote system.','For local enrollment effects, an appropriate uniqueness constraint and transactional operation record can enforce one logical enrollment. For emitted events, durable outbox/consumer deduplication patterns help coordinate local commits with delivery. Full database transaction mechanics are taught later; the API contract must still expose pending, succeeded, conflicted and unresolved outcomes honestly.'),
 section('Separate replay, conflict and compensation','A replay should return the documented logical result without repeating the business effect. A conflict should identify a safe repair action without exposing another actor’s stored response. An operation that succeeded remotely but failed later in a workflow may require compensation, such as a refund request, not pretending the original call never happened.','The fixture uses an in-memory dictionary and canonical JSON to illustrate sequential replay and payload conflict only. It is not durable, concurrent, cryptographic authentication or a payment service. The text’s production requirements deliberately exceed what this small model can guarantee.')],
 'import json\nrecords = {}\neffects = []\ndef submit(actor, key, payload):\n    scope = (actor, key)\n    fingerprint = json.dumps(payload, sort_keys=True, separators=(",", ":"))\n    if scope in records:\n        old, result = records[scope]\n        return result if old == fingerprint else "payload_conflict"\n    effects.append("charge-demo")\n    result = "payment-1"\n    records[scope] = (fingerprint, result)\n    return result\nprint(submit(7, "retry-1", {"cents": 1200}))\nprint(submit(7, "retry-1", {"cents": 1200}))\nprint(submit(7, "retry-1", {"cents": 1300}))\nprint(len(effects))','payment-1\npayment-1\npayload_conflict\n1',
 'Model sequential identical replay and conflicting payload behavior for a single actor.',['Scope the fixture record to actor and key.','Compare a canonical representation of the payment fields.','Verify that the same logical request reuses its result while changed details conflict.'],'Only one fixture effect is recorded. This is not a crash-safe or concurrent guarantee; durable coordination and provider reconciliation are required before using the design for real payments.',
 ['A volatile map is not durable multi-replica deduplication.','Recording a key alone cannot close an independent provider crash gap.','A new operation id after timeout may create a duplicate effect.'],['Scope, fingerprint, persist and reconcile logical operations.','Specify concurrent/in-progress and retention behavior.','Treat compensation as an explicit business operation.'],['http-semantics','openapi']);
add('rest-deadlines-limits','m3.c2','Budget time, retries and capacity across services',
 'A logistics dashboard calls a courier API that sometimes stalls. A payment service must remain available even when analytics exports are expensive. Good API reliability makes waiting and resource use bounded, and tells clients how to respond when limits are reached.',[
 section('Design one overall budget instead of independent waits','Set connection/read/overall deadlines appropriate to the request, and propagate the remaining budget to downstream calls. If A gives up after ten seconds while B starts a thirty-second call to C, abandoned work can continue consuming resources. Cancellation is best effort and does not prove remote writes rolled back.','Assign retry ownership. Retry only eligible failures and operation semantics, with bounded attempts, backoff and jitter. Permanent validation/authentication errors require repair rather than repetition. Several layers each retrying three times can multiply total dependency traffic rather than improve resilience.'),
 section('Rate limits and overload policy express fairness','A token bucket can allow short bursts while limiting sustained use. Scope limits to meaningful actors or tenants so one caller cannot consume shared capacity. A 429 response can communicate a documented limit and Retry-After guidance; Retry-After can be a delay or HTTP date, so a real client needs correct parsing and clock policy.','Separate expensive or lower-priority workloads from essential operations where business policy permits. Use bounded queues and admission control, not unlimited buffering. A circuit breaker can stop repeated waits against a failing dependency and probe for recovery; it does not authorize requests, repair invalid data or guarantee that every failure is transient.'),
 section('Tell clients what is known and measure real behavior','Expose documented quota information where useful without treating rate-limit headers as permanent permission. Choose fallback behavior explicitly: stale tracking may be acceptable if labelled, while an unknown payment decision must not become a successful charge. Measure latency/error rates and capacity before copying numeric defaults from a lesson.','The worked retry schedule uses fixed synthetic waits to demonstrate a budget decision. It is not an HTTP date parser, jitter generator, worker or cancellation mechanism. The important contract is that the client stops when the remaining budget cannot support another attempt rather than sleeping past its caller’s deadline.')],
 'budget = 5\nwaits = [1, 2, 4]\nelapsed = 0\nfor wait in waits:\n    if elapsed + wait >= budget:\n        print("stop", elapsed)\n        break\n    elapsed += wait\n    print("retry_after", wait)\nprint("budget", budget)', 'retry_after 1\nretry_after 2\nstop 3\nbudget 5',
 'Bound a synthetic retry-wait schedule by a caller’s overall five-unit budget.',['Accumulate the already spent waiting time.','Check whether the next proposed wait fits before scheduling it.','Stop rather than schedule a wait that exhausts the remaining budget.'],'The example accounts only for these fixed waits; real policies also budget attempt durations, connection setup and processing overhead. It does not imply that any retry is safe for an arbitrary business write.',
 ['Independent timeouts can leave work running beyond the caller budget.','Rate limiting is not object authorization.','A circuit breaker is not a license to return fabricated success.'],['Propagate bounded budgets and assign retry ownership.','Choose fairness, bursts, queues and priorities deliberately.','Make fallbacks truthful and operation-specific.'],['http-semantics']);
add('rest-cache-concurrency','m3.c3','Use caching and validators without losing correctness',
 'A public product image, an editable workspace and a private account balance have different reuse rules. HTTP Foundations explained cache directives. Here the design question is which representation can be reused by whom, for how long, and whether an update may overwrite a newer edit.',[
 section('Give each resource a suitable freshness contract','Immutable public assets can use long freshness lifetimes when a changed asset receives a new URL. Frequently edited product prices need shorter lifetimes, revalidation or a reliable invalidation policy. Private balances and profiles need explicit sensitive-response rules; a public CDN cache key shared by every signed-in user can leak representations.','Vary communicates request fields used to choose a representation, such as Accept-Language. It is not a replacement for tenant/user authorization or an automatic safe private cache key. no-cache permits storage but requires validation before reuse; no-store forbids storage under the policy. Neither directive erases copies already obtained by an endpoint.'),
 section('Use validators for reads and conditional writes','An ETag identifies a representation version. If-None-Match on a GET can revalidate a cached representation and receive 304 when appropriate. If-Match on an update can require the client’s known version; a mismatch normally produces 412 under that precondition contract, avoiding a silent lost update.','The version check and write need one atomic concurrency rule in the real service. Comparing a tag, then allowing another update before writing, leaves a race. A cache validator is not permission: the actor must still be allowed to read or modify the resource. Strong/weak validator rules differ, so do not use a weak validator as if it satisfied every write precondition.'),
 section('Prevent synchronized rebuilds and stale derived data','A popular report expiring for every caller at once can trigger a cache stampede. Request coalescing, staggered expiry or a carefully bounded stale-while-revalidate strategy can reduce duplicate rebuilding where stale results are acceptable. Invalidation must follow the data’s business rules; a time-to-live alone can leave edited records stale.','The fixture models sequential conditional writes and explicitly leaves the current state unchanged on a stale tag. It does not implement HTTP entity-tag parsing, a distributed cache or an atomic database compare-and-set. Those limitations should be visible rather than hidden behind a passing toy example.')],
 'document = {"version": 2, "title": "Current"}\ndef update(expected, title):\n    if expected != document["version"]:\n        return 412\n    document["title"] = title\n    document["version"] += 1\n    return 200\nprint(update(1, "Stale edit"))\nprint(document["title"])\nprint(update(2, "Fresh edit"))\nprint(document["version"])','412\nCurrent\n200\n3',
 'Reject an outdated edit and then accept an edit based on the current representation version.',['Compare the expected and current fixture version.','Return a failed precondition without mutating state for a stale client.','Apply a current edit and advance the version.'],'The stale change is refused and the valid one succeeds. A production implementation needs atomic verification/write plus real validator and authorization rules.',
 ['An ETag is not an access token.','A read-then-write check can still race without atomic enforcement.','A shared public cache must not mix private user representations.'],['Specify freshness, variation and privacy per resource.','Use correct read validation and write preconditions.','Design invalidation and rebuild behavior for the workload.'],['http-cache','http-semantics']);
add('rest-webhooks','m4.c1','Receive authenticated, repeatable and ordered events',
 'A payment provider notifies a shop that a payment succeeded. The endpoint is public so the provider can reach it, but “publicly reachable” must not mean “trust any supplied event.” Delivery can be duplicated, delayed or out of order.',[
 section('Authenticate the delivery before trusting its meaning','Follow the provider’s signature protocol over the exact required bytes, with the correct secret/key, timestamp window and constant-time verification where specified. Parsing and reserializing JSON before checking a signature can change the bytes and invalidate the protocol. TLS protects the transport but does not prove any caller is the provider.','Only acknowledge success according to a durable acceptance policy. If the event must survive a process crash, persist validated delivery/processing state before reporting the applicable success. A fast 2xx that discards unrecorded work can lose events. Keep signature material and customer data out of routine logs.'),
 section('Separate duplicate delivery from repeated business effect','Use provider event ids and a scoped durable inbox/deduplication record. Reprocessing the same delivery must not repeatedly confirm an order or grant credit. A deduplication record must be coordinated with the local business effect so a crash between “seen” and “applied” does not lose the work.','Do not assume arrival order is business order. An older out-for-delivery event arriving after delivered should not regress the tracking state. Use provider sequence/version or an explicitly reviewed state-transition rule; timestamps alone may not settle all distributed ordering cases. Unknown or conflicting events need a reconciliation policy.'),
 section('Plan recovery beyond the happy delivery path','A long outage can exceed a provider’s retry window. Reconcile against an authoritative status/query feed and retain enough event history to resolve missed or ambiguous operations. When sending webhooks, bound retry attempts and delay, isolate tenants and route exhausted deliveries to an operational recovery queue rather than filling one infinite queue.','The fixture models event id and monotonic version checks using synthetic records. It does not verify signatures, provide durable storage or prove a real provider’s ordering guarantees. Its outputs establish the transition policy only under its declared version assumptions.',json({event_id:'evt-9',shipment_id:'ship-3',version:3,status:'delivered'}))],
 'seen = set()\nstate = {"version": 0, "status": "created"}\ndef apply(event):\n    if event["id"] in seen:\n        return "duplicate"\n    seen.add(event["id"])\n    if event["version"] <= state["version"]:\n        return "older"\n    state.update(version=event["version"], status=event["status"])\n    return "applied"\na = {"id": "evt-9", "version": 3, "status": "delivered"}\nb = {"id": "evt-8", "version": 2, "status": "out_for_delivery"}\nprint(apply(a), apply(a), apply(b))\nprint(state["status"])','applied duplicate older\ndelivered',
 'Keep a synthetic shipment delivered despite a duplicate and a late older event.',['Detect an already seen fixture event id.','Apply only events newer than the current declared provider version.','Inspect the final state after an out-of-order event.'],'The status does not regress. Real code must authenticate, persist and coordinate the inbox/effect, and must not assume this provider version field exists without a contract.',
 ['TLS alone does not authenticate the webhook sender’s business identity.','Marking seen before an uncoordinated effect can lose work after a crash.','Delivery order is not automatically event order.'],['Verify provenance before processing.','Design durable deduplication and state ordering.','Reconcile events that delivery retries cannot recover.'],['http-semantics','openapi']);
add('rest-partial-failures','m4.c1','Model partial failures and recoverable workflows',
 'A checkout creates an order, charges payment and reserves stock across independent systems. If the third step fails, the earlier steps do not automatically roll back. A professional API exposes a workflow’s actual state and its recovery plan.',[
 section('Distinguish atomic local work from distributed progress','A database transaction can coordinate appropriate local changes, but a remote payment API and message broker do not automatically join it. A saga coordinates a sequence of operations and compensating actions. A compensation such as refund initiation is a new business action that can itself fail or remain pending; it is not literal time travel.','Use explicit states such as pending_payment, payment_confirmed, awaiting_stock, compensation_pending and completed. Choose which are public, and let clients inspect a stable operation resource. Do not collapse “unknown” into “success” or erase history to make a failed workflow look as if it never started.'),
 section('Close publication gaps with durable intent','If an order commits locally but publishing its event fails, a transactional outbox records the event intent with the local transaction. A separate dispatcher delivers it with retry; consumers still need idempotency because delivery can repeat. An outbox does not promise exactly-once processing by every independent system.','An inbound event uses an analogous durable inbox/processing policy. Keep ownership and recovery states explicit. Unbounded retry queues can crowd out other tenants, so apply limits and operational escalation/reconciliation paths. These patterns are presented as contract-level design; database implementation belongs to the integration subtrack.'),
 section('Validate dependencies and degrade honestly','A provider returning 200 with an empty or invalid body is not proof that a credit check found no risks. Validate both protocol and domain response before making a critical decision; choose fail-closed or pending/manual recovery according to the business contract. For a noncritical dashboard panel, labelled unavailability or stale data may be acceptable instead of failing the whole page.','The example records a fixture state after a stock rejection following payment success. It does not call a provider or reverse money. “compensation_pending” intentionally avoids claiming a completed refund without corresponding evidence.')],
 'workflow = {"payment": "confirmed", "stock": "rejected"}\nif workflow["payment"] == "confirmed" and workflow["stock"] == "rejected":\n    workflow["status"] = "compensation_pending"\n    workflow["next_action"] = "request_refund"\nelse:\n    workflow["status"] = "pending"\nprint(workflow["status"])\nprint(workflow["next_action"])\nprint(workflow["payment"])','compensation_pending\nrequest_refund\nconfirmed',
 'Represent an unfinished compensation rather than reporting a refund that has not occurred.',['Inspect the independently recorded payment and stock outcomes.','Choose an explicit pending compensation state.','Preserve the confirmed payment evidence and the next recovery action.'],'The original payment remains confirmed until a separate refund operation is verified. The fixture is a state decision, not a real saga engine or financial side effect.',
 ['A local rollback cannot undo every remote API call.','200 plus an invalid body is not a valid critical-domain result.','An outbox still needs retry, deduplication and recovery policy.'],['Make partial progress and uncertainty observable.','Use durable intent to close local commit/publication gaps.','Choose truthful fallback and compensation states.'],['http-semantics']);
add('rest-secrets-contract','m4.c2','Protect credentials and finalize a reviewable API contract',
 'A backend needs a payment-provider key, but the mobile client should never receive that shared credential. Secret handling, minimum scope and safe observability are part of the API contract. Finish by reviewing the order workflow as a whole rather than checking endpoints independently.',[
 section('Keep credentials at the correct trust boundary','Store outbound provider credentials in appropriately protected server configuration or a secret manager and send them only to the intended provider over verified HTTPS. Do not bundle a company master key in a mobile app or public JavaScript. A backend-mediated capability still needs authentication, authorization, quotas and abuse controls; moving the key alone does not make every operation safe.','Support scoped keys with least privilege where the provider/service permits it. For inbound API credentials that only need verification, store an appropriate nonrecoverable verifier rather than plaintext, with a design suited to high-entropy keys. Outbound credentials must remain usable to call their provider and therefore require protected recoverable storage; these cases must not be conflated.'),
 section('Rotate and revoke with a tested lifecycle','A key accidentally published in Git history must be treated as compromised: revoke/rotate it, investigate exposure and remove unsafe references. Deleting a line in the latest commit does not revoke copied credentials or erase history. Do not print real secret values while debugging or building review packs.','Planned rotation can overlap old/new credentials for a bounded migration, observe usage and retire the old key. Redact Authorization, Cookie and known secret properties in logs; avoid ad hoc “log every header” debug output. A hashed verifier or redacted log does not eliminate the need for permissions and controlled backups.'),
 section('Review one business contract across boundaries','For the order API, review resource identity, method, request schema, writable fields, owner permissions, allowed transitions, conflicts, duplicate-operation identity, status polling, paging and error envelope. Add contract tests for both accepted and forbidden cases, and define how unavailable dependencies affect state. OpenAPI documents part of this contract but cannot execute every business invariant.','The fixture projects an allowlisted diagnostic record. It proves the shown output omits token and card fields; it is not a general recursive redaction library. Test nested data and new fields before adopting any logging policy, and keep verification artifacts private when they contain instructional answer keys.',json({request_id:'req-demo-12',method:'POST',path:'/orders',status:201}))],
 'import json\nrequest = {"request_id": "req-demo-12", "method": "POST", "path": "/orders", "status": 201, "authorization": "SYNTHETIC-NOT-A-CREDENTIAL", "card": "SYNTHETIC-NOT-A-CARD"}\nallowed = ("request_id", "method", "path", "status")\nlog = {k: request[k] for k in allowed}\nassert "authorization" not in log and "card" not in log\nprint(json.dumps(log, sort_keys=True))','{"method": "POST", "path": "/orders", "request_id": "req-demo-12", "status": 201}',
 'Produce a useful allowlisted diagnostic entry without copying synthetic credential/payment fields.',['Select only the known nonsecret operational fields.','Check that the excluded fields are not present.','Serialize the reviewed log projection deterministically.'],'The diagnostic still links method, path, status and request id. Production policy also needs nested-data handling, retention, access controls and tests for newly introduced fields.',
 ['Removing a leaked key from the newest commit does not revoke it.','Inbound credential verifiers and outbound usable secrets need different storage designs.','An unrestricted backend proxy can still expose a paid capability to abuse.'],['Keep credentials scoped, protected and revocable.','Use allowlisted diagnostics and test the actual output.','Review the complete business contract, including failure and ownership paths.'],['openapi','http-semantics']);
// Illustrative wire request; protocol framing/transport remain the HTTP prerequisite.
const requestBody=JSON.stringify({product_id:'prod-7',quantity:2});
lessons[0].sections[0].snippet={lang:'http',code:'POST /orders HTTP/1.1\r\nHost: shop.example.test\r\nContent-Type: application/json\r\nContent-Length: '+Buffer.byteLength(requestBody,'utf8')+'\r\n\r\n'+requestBody};
